      "text": "Shift 2 on 2026-09-14 ran at 88.4% of wet-end speed target, and downtime explains only a small part of that.\n\n**Downtime by reason (chart rendered):**\n- Order change: 3.25 min (0.68% of shift)\n- Raw material (roll): 2.60 min (0.54%)\n- Missed splice: 2.23 min (0.47%)\n- Knife / slitter jam: 1.07 min (0.22%)\n- Stacker: 0.97 min (0.20%)\n\nTotal recorded downtime is 2.11% of the shift. Operator stops are 1.89% and maintenance is 0.22%, both under the 2.5% targets. Order change is the largest single reason, but the stops are short.\n\n**What else drives the slowdown:**\n- **Speed vs. target:** All 24 wet-end runs were below their grade target, so the shortfall is spread across the shift rather than caused by a few stops. Stops alone do not account for the 88.4% attainment. Grade-target performance is the next thing to look at.\n- **Trim:** 3.22% overall, just under the 3.25% flag, but 23 of 24 setups were above the trim target.\n- **Dry-end rejects:** 1.12% of gross area, above the 1% flag. Warp is the largest reject reason at about 9,439 sq ft.\n- **Shear:** 0.63%, within the 0.4\u20131% guidance band.\n\nDowntime is a minor contributor. The more likely drivers are the grade-specific speed targets and the trim and warp losses. I have not checked the setup-level or grade-level detail, so those causes are not confirmed."
